  SELECT * FROM (VALUES
    -- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    -- ALGERIA (DZA) — All 7 Sectors
    -- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ('DZA', 'digital_infrastructure', 'Digital Infrastructure',
      $$Algeria's digital infrastructure combines fiber backbone expansion, mobile network modernization, data center development, and digital public services supporting economic diversification.$$,
      $$Algeria is expanding digital infrastructure including fiber backbone networks connecting major cities, 4G/5G mobile network deployment, and government investment in data center capacity. E-government services support administrative modernization. Submarine cable connectivity through Alval and other systems provides international bandwidth. The country is developing digital economy frameworks and technology parks. Digital inclusion programs target interior regions. The sector represents opportunity for connectivity infrastructure, cloud services, digital public infrastructure, and technology adoption supporting post-hydrocarbon economic diversification.$$,
      58, 68, 1, 'explorer'),
    ('DZA', 'fintech_digital_finance', 'Fintech and Digital Finance',
      $$Algeria's financial sector is evolving through banking modernization, payment infrastructure development, regulatory reforms, and digital financial services expansion.$$,
      $$Algeria's financial sector operates within a state-influenced framework undergoing modernization including banking technology upgrades, payment system development, and expanding digital channels. Mobile money and digital payment platforms are in early growth stages. The central bank supports payment system modernization and fintech regulatory frameworks. Banking infrastructure serves urban centers with expanding reach. Foreign exchange controls and financial sector reforms influence development trajectory. The sector is positioned for digital banking expansion, payment infrastructure investment, and financial inclusion services supporting broader economic modernization and youth employment.$$,
      52, 65, 2, 'explorer'),
    ('DZA', 'energy_renewables', 'Energy and Renewables',
      $$Algeria's energy sector is anchored by substantial oil and gas resources, with expanding renewable energy programs targeting solar deployment and export potential.$$,
      $$Algeria is a major oil and gas producer and exporter, with significant hydrocarbon reserves supporting domestic energy needs and export revenues. The country possesses exceptional solar energy potential in Saharan regions and is developing large-scale solar projects for domestic generation and potential regional export. Government renewable energy targets aim to diversify the energy mix and reduce domestic gas consumption, freeing resources for export. Power generation and grid infrastructure support industrial and residential demand. The sector represents strategic opportunity for solar energy development, green hydrogen potential, and regional energy integration positioning Algeria as a renewable energy supplier.$$,
      72, 75, 3, 'explorer'),
    ('DZA', 'agriculture_agribusiness', 'Agriculture and Agribusiness',
      $$Algeria's agriculture includes cereals, fruits, vegetables, dates, livestock, and food security programs addressing import dependence and water constraints.$$,
      $$Algeria's agricultural sector produces cereals, fruits, vegetables, dates, and livestock, with substantial food imports required to meet domestic demand. The country faces water scarcity and arable land constraints influencing productivity. Government programs support agricultural development, irrigation investment, and food security initiatives. Dates and Mediterranean produce offer export potential. Saharan agriculture and oasis farming contribute to production. The sector represents opportunity for agro-processing investment, modern farming techniques, irrigation technology, and value chain development supporting food security and rural development in a water-constrained environment.$$,
      55, 62, 4, 'explorer'),
    ('DZA', 'mining_critical_minerals', 'Mining and Critical Minerals',
      $$Algeria's mining sector includes phosphates, iron ore, zinc, gold, and exploration for critical minerals complementing dominant hydrocarbon production.$$,
      $$Algeria produces phosphates for fertilizer and export, alongside iron ore, zinc, lead, and gold. The country's geology supports exploration for additional mineral resources including critical minerals. Mining sector development occurs alongside dominant oil and gas industry. Government mining sector reforms aim to attract investment while strengthening regulatory frameworks. Mineral beneficiation and value-added processing represent development priorities. The sector represents opportunity for mineral exploration, phosphate sector expansion, and resource diversification supporting economic development beyond hydrocarbons.$$,
      60, 65, 5, 'explorer'),
    ('DZA', 'logistics_trade', 'Logistics and Trade',
      $$Algeria's logistics infrastructure includes Mediterranean ports, road and rail networks, trans-Saharan corridors, and positioning for regional trade and energy transit.$$,
      $$Algeria operates Mediterranean port facilities including Algiers, Oran, and Annaba serving imports, exports including hydrocarbons, and potential regional trade. Road and rail networks connect coastal regions and extend into Saharan zones. Trans-Saharan transport corridors link Algeria to sub-Saharan Africa. Energy pipeline infrastructure connects production to Mediterranean export terminals. African Continental Free Trade Area participation supports trade integration. The sector represents opportunity for port modernization, transport corridor development, logistics services, and regional connectivity infrastructure leveraging Algeria's geographic position between Mediterranean and Sahel regions.$$,
      62, 68, 6, 'explorer'),
    ('DZA', 'tourism_hospitality', 'Tourism and Hospitality',
      $$Algeria's tourism potential includes Sahara tourism, Roman heritage sites, Mediterranean coast, cultural assets, and destination development addressing security and infrastructure.$$,
      $$Algeria possesses significant tourism assets including Sahara desert landscapes, UNESCO World Heritage sites with Roman ruins at Djémila and Timgad, Mediterranean coastline, and cultural heritage. The tourism sector faces infrastructure, security, and visa policy constraints limiting development. Government tourism sector reforms aim to attract investment and expand visitor access. Hospitality infrastructure exists in major cities and emerging tourist zones. Domestic and diaspora tourism support baseline activity. The sector represents long-term opportunity for destination development, heritage tourism, Saharan tourism infrastructure, and aviation connectivity aligned with security improvements and investment climate reforms.$$,
      48, 62, 7, 'explorer'),

    -- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    -- LIBYA (LBY) — All 7 Sectors
    -- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ('LBY', 'digital_infrastructure', 'Digital Infrastructure',
      $$Libya's digital infrastructure is recovering through telecommunications network rehabilitation, mobile service restoration, connectivity investment, and digital services rebuilding.$$,
      $$Libya's telecommunications sector is undergoing recovery and rehabilitation following conflict disruption, with mobile networks expanding coverage and quality. Fiber backbone and international submarine cable connectivity support improving internet access. E-government services and digital identity programs are in early recovery stages. Infrastructure damage and security conditions affect consistent service delivery. Government and private sector investment targets connectivity restoration and expansion. The sector represents opportunity for infrastructure rehabilitation, network modernization, digital services development, and technology adoption supporting broader national reconstruction and economic recovery.$$,
      45, 68, 1, 'explorer'),
    ('LBY', 'fintech_digital_finance', 'Fintech and Digital Finance',
      $$Libya's financial sector faces challenges including banking liquidity, parallel exchange rates, cash economy dominance, and digital financial services at early stages.$$,
      $$Libya's financial sector operates in a complex environment with banking system challenges including liquidity constraints, parallel foreign exchange markets, and heavy reliance on cash transactions. Mobile money and digital payment platforms are emerging slowly. The Central Bank and financial institutions face reconstruction and reunification challenges. Banking infrastructure serves major cities with limited rural reach. Financial sector stabilization and digital channel development represent priorities as political and economic conditions improve. The sector is positioned for gradual digital banking expansion, payment infrastructure investment, and financial inclusion services contingent on broader stability and governance improvements.$$,
      40, 65, 2, 'explorer'),
    ('LBY', 'energy_renewables', 'Energy and Renewables',
      $$Libya's energy sector is dominated by substantial oil reserves, with renewable energy potential including solar resources and power sector rehabilitation needs.$$,
      $$Libya possesses significant oil and gas reserves and production capacity, with energy sector recovery ongoing following conflict-related disruption. The country has exceptional solar energy potential for domestic generation and potential export. Power generation infrastructure requires rehabilitation and expansion to meet demand reliably. Oil revenue dominance creates fiscal dependence while offering capital for diversification. Government renewable energy planning targets solar deployment. The sector represents opportunity for oil sector stabilization, solar energy development, power infrastructure rehabilitation, and energy sector modernization supporting economic recovery and fiscal sustainability.$$,
      60, 70, 3, 'explorer'),
    ('LBY', 'agriculture_agribusiness', 'Agriculture and Agribusiness',
      $$Libya's agriculture includes olives, dates, cereals, livestock, and food security initiatives addressing water scarcity, import dependence, and conflict impacts.$$,
      $$Libya's agricultural sector produces olives, dates, cereals, and livestock in coastal and oasis regions, with substantial food imports meeting domestic demand. Water scarcity, arid climate, and conflict disruption constrain production. Government programs support agricultural development and food security. Irrigation investment and modern farming techniques could enhance productivity. The sector represents opportunity for agricultural investment, agro-processing, water-efficient farming, and food security programs supporting import substitution and rural development as stability improves and investment climate strengthens.$$,
      42, 58, 4, 'explorer'),
    ('LBY', 'mining_critical_minerals', 'Mining and Critical Minerals',
      $$Libya's mining sector is limited beyond hydrocarbons, with gypsum, limestone production, and geological potential for mineral exploration and development.$$,
      $$Libya's mineral sector beyond oil and gas includes gypsum, limestone, and construction materials supporting domestic demand. The country's geology may support additional mineral resources, though exploration has been limited. Dominant oil sector and security conditions constrain mining sector development. Mining regulatory frameworks and investment climate require strengthening. The sector represents limited traditional mining opportunity in the near term, with longer-term potential for geological surveys, exploration, and responsible mining development aligned with broader economic diversification and governance improvements.$$,
      35, 52, 5, 'explorer'),
    ('LBY', 'logistics_trade', 'Logistics and Trade',
      $$Libya's logistics infrastructure includes Mediterranean ports, road networks, conflict-affected corridors, and positioning for North-South trade and energy transit.$$,
      $$Libya operates Mediterranean port facilities at Tripoli, Benghazi, and Misrata supporting imports, oil exports, and regional trade, with infrastructure rehabilitation ongoing. Road networks connect coastal cities and extend into Saharan regions. Trans-Saharan trade routes historically linked Libya to sub-Saharan markets. Conflict and security conditions affect logistics infrastructure functionality. African Continental Free Trade Area participation offers trade integration potential. The sector represents opportunity for port rehabilitation, transport corridor recovery, border trade facilitation, and logistics services supporting economic reconstruction and regional connectivity as stability improves.$$,
      48, 65, 6, 'explorer'),
    ('LBY', 'tourism_hospitality', 'Tourism and Hospitality',
      $$Libya's tourism potential includes Roman heritage sites, Sahara landscapes, Mediterranean coast, and destination development contingent on security and stability improvements.$$,
      $$Libya possesses significant tourism assets including UNESCO World Heritage sites with Roman ruins at Leptis Magna and Sabratha, Sahara desert landscapes, Mediterranean coastline, and historical heritage. Tourism sector development has been severely constrained by conflict and security conditions. Hospitality infrastructure exists in major cities but requires investment and rehabilitation. Destination potential remains unrealized pending stability, security improvements, and investment climate reforms. The sector represents long-term opportunity for heritage tourism development, destination recovery, and infrastructure investment aligned with broader national stabilization and reconstruction.$$,
      38, 58, 7, 'explorer'),

    -- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
